PROMPT_CASE_RUN review status=Completed error=None result=Some("**Complete.** Read-only correctness review of `src/limit.rs` is done — no files changed. The file exists but is only 4 lines, and the workspace contains no callers, tests, or `Cargo.toml`, so findings rest on the code's own stated intent.\n\n## Findings\n\n### 1. HIGH — Off-by-one: returns `MAX_LINES + 1` lines, not `MAX_LINES`\n- **Evidence:** `src/limit.rs:1` — `pub const MAX_LINES: usize = 3;` and `src/limit.rs:3` — `(0..=MAX_LINES).map(|i| lines[i].to_string()).collect()`\n- The inclusive range `0..=MAX_LINES` yields indices `0,1,2,3` — four elements — while `MAX_LINES` and the function name `first_lines` state the contract is at most 3 lines.\n- **Failing input:** `first_lines(&[\"a\", \"b\", \"c\", \"d\", \"e\"])` returns `[\"a\", \"b\", \"c\", \"d\"]` instead of the first 3.\n\n### 2. HIGH — Reachable panic for inputs shorter than 4 elements\n- **Evidence:** `src/limit.rs:3` — `lines[i]` direct slice indexing with no bounds guard.\n- A \"first N\" helper must tolerate short inputs, but any slice shorter than 4 indexes out of bounds.\n- **Failing inputs:** `first_lines(&[])` panics at index 0; `first_lines(&[\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"])` panics at index 3.\n\n**Smallest fix for both** (one line, saturating by construction):\n\n```rust\npub fn first_lines(lines: &[&str]) -> Vec<String> {\n    lines.iter().take(MAX_LINES).map(|s| s.to_string()).collect()\n}\n```\n\nThe two defects are independent: changing `..=` to `..` fixes only #1 and leaves the panic reachable.\n\n## Verified correct\n- No overflow/underflow: `0..=usize::MAX` is legal for `RangeInclusive` even at extreme constant values.\n- The `&str` → `String` conversion itself is correct; no error handling, state, or concurrency surface to mishandle.\n\n## Coverage limits\n- No callers or tests exist in the workspace (`src/` contains only this file), so intent is inferred from the names `MAX_LINES`/`first_lines` rather than usage.\n- rust-analyzer diagnostics were unavailable (warning health); both findings are established directly from unambiguous Rust semantics (inclusive-range cardinality and slice-index panics).")
